<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While real and synthesized documents share the same underlying concept, they differ in multiple ways that merit closer examination. We categorize these differences into a taxonomy of relations using a small ontology. Table <a href="#A2.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ B.1 Analyze concepts in documents ‣ Appendix B Additional analysis of synthesized samples ‣ Synthetic bootstrapped pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> illustrates several relationship types, highlighting how synthesized data can reflect multiple facets that vary from real data. These relations range from scope-based distinctions (e.g., specific vs. general), to causal connections (e.g., corruption leading to reform), and to contrastive contrasts (e.g., Constitution articles vs. Articles of Confederation). This diversity demonstrates the rich variation structure that the synthesizer captures and learns.</p>
</div>
